  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAnother quiet and cool day across the area with upper level \nridging over the area and surface high pressure off to our east.\nTemperatures as of 2 PM were in the lower to mid 50s\n\nA shortwave trough over the Southern Plains will continue to \ndive southeast tonight into Monday, allowing upper level ridging\nto further amplify across the forecast area on Monday. In \naddition, we'll have strengthening southerly winds ahead of \ndeveloping low pressure east of the Rockies. This will \ncontribute to a warming trend through mid-week, with highs in \nthe mid 50s Monday, mid to upper 50s Tuesday, and fairly \nwidespread 60s by Wednesday. The aforementioned winds will be \npretty gusty, especially on Tuesday and Wednesday, when EPS mean\ngusts are in the 30-40+ mph range (highest in northeast NE). \nLooking at model soundings, winds at the top of the mixed layer \ncould even approach 50 mph, though very few ensemble members \nshow winds that strong reaching the surface. These winds could \nlead to some fire concerns, primarily in unharvested fields, \nthough with RH currently favored to remain above 40%, those \nconcerns will be tamped down a bit.\n\nThe aforementioned low will be pushing through the the Dakotas \non Wednesday, dragging a cold front through the forecast area \nWednesday evening. In addition, some weak shortwave energy will \nbe rounding the back side of the low and eject toward the \nforecast area out of western KS. Guidance still hints at some \nspotty precip as the front and this wave move through, though \nthe better moisture plume continues to trend toward remaining to\nour south and east, likely limiting any precip to a few \nhundredths, if any. Currently looking at a 20-30% chance, mainly\nnear and east of the Missouri river.\n\nAttention then turns to the stronger system progged to bring \nmore widespread rain Friday and into the weekend. Guidance \nremains in good agreement of a trough digging into the western \nCONUS by Thursday, with the axis passing through the Rockies \nFriday, and through the forecast area on Saturday. Ahead of the \ntrough, a surface low will spin up east of the Rockies with \nstrengthening warm air and moisture advection leading to at \nleast some spotty showers on Friday. Still some spread on track \nand timing of the low thereafter, but the general trend is a bit\nfaster and farther north, meaning we'd see just a quick shot of\nshowers and maybe a few storms Friday night into Saturday as \nthe cold front moves through and any snow on the northwest side \nof the low would stay well to our northwest.",
